From: Coram Engineering <eng@coram.ai>
Subject: [PATCH] fmp4mux: do not skip the first stream on a late GOP

c3740cd6 ("fmp4mux: Fix handling of stream with late single GOP") added
`late_gop` to the early return of drain_buffers_one_stream() so that a stream
whose queued GOPs all start after the current fragment end is skipped and the
*other* streams can still be drained.

Applying that to the first stream is fatal. The first stream is the one that
decides the chunk end PTS. If it drains nothing, drain() finds no last GOP,
`stream_after_chunk` is evaluated against `chunk_start_pts + chunk_duration`
(a later boundary than the `fragment_end_pts` used to declare the stream
late), so the escape does not apply and drain() returns
AGGREGATOR_FLOW_NEED_DATA. No chunk is started, no fragment is started,
nothing is pushed, and the input queue grows without bound. `late_gop` is
cleared and re-armed on the next cycle, so this repeats forever.

Before c3740cd6 the early return fired only on an empty queue, so the loop
always ran and the first stream reached its dedicated fallback:

    (gop.final_end_pts || ...) && (gop.end_pts <= dequeue_end_pts
        || (gops.is_empty() && chunk_end_pts.is_none()))

whose comment describes precisely this case ("no GOPs were dequeued yet and
this is the first stream ... if no GOP ends between the last chunk of the
fragment and the fragment duration"). That fallback drained the late GOP and
output kept flowing.

Restrict the late-GOP skip to non-first streams so the first stream reaches
that fallback again. The assertion c3740cd6 guards is already satisfied in
chunk mode via `settings.chunk_duration.is_some()`, so the panic fix is
unaffected.

Observed on a live H.264+AAC pipeline (fragment-duration=2s,
chunk-duration=100ms) where a GOP began 36.76us after the fragment end: 373
drains entered in 60s, 385 NEED_DATA returns, zero chunks or fragments
started, and over three minutes of GOPs queued and still growing.

Also log the otherwise silent early return, which is the only no-buffer exit
that emitted no trace at all and made this hard to attribute.
--- a/mux/fmp4/src/fmp4mux/imp.rs
+++ b/mux/fmp4/src/fmp4mux/imp.rs
@@ -1981,10 +1981,27 @@
         );
 
         let mut gops = Vec::with_capacity(stream.queued_gops.len());
-        if stream.queued_gops.is_empty() || stream.late_gop {
+        // Skipping a stream with a late GOP lets the *other* streams be drained, which is
+        // the point of `late_gop`. It must not be applied to the first stream: draining
+        // nothing there means no chunk end PTS is decided, drain() takes the
+        // AGGREGATOR_FLOW_NEED_DATA path and the muxer stops producing output entirely
+        // while its queue grows without bound.
+        //
+        // The first stream (chunk_end_pts == None) has a dedicated fallback further down
+        // that pushes its GOP even when the GOP ends after the dequeue end PTS, which is
+        // exactly the late-GOP case. Let it run instead of returning early here.
+        if stream.queued_gops.is_empty() || (stream.late_gop && chunk_end_pts.is_some()) {
+            gst::debug!(
+                CAT,
+                obj = stream.sinkpad,
+                "Draining nothing for this stream (no queued GOPs: {}, late GOP: {})",
+                stream.queued_gops.is_empty(),
+                stream.late_gop,
+            );
             stream.late_gop = false;
             return Ok(gops);
         }
+        stream.late_gop = false;
 
         // In manual-split mode, drain everything until the split-now event as requested.
         if settings.manual_split {
